package dev.myair.canvas

import dev.myair.store.Canvas

/** The OpenUI system prompt of the canvas library (`canvas/system-prompt.txt`, generated by `canvas-web`'s build), read once. */
val openUiPrompt: String by lazy {
    checkNotNull(Canvas::class.java.getResourceAsStream("/canvas/system-prompt.txt")) { "canvas/system-prompt.txt missing; run npm run build in canvas-web" }
        .use { String(it.readAllBytes(), Charsets.UTF_8) }
}

/**
 * My Air's rules for writing to a canvas, which come before the OpenUI reference: what the canvas is for, how the tools
 * name a canvas and answer, and where OpenUI's own prompt does not apply (it was written for a model whose whole reply
 * is UI).
 */
fun canvasRules(): String = """
    # My Air canvas

    A My Air canvas folder has a tab in the IDE that renders OpenUI Lang documents, called frames, next to its manager's terminal.
    The terminal stays the place for the conversation; the canvas holds results meant to be looked at, compared or clicked:
    summaries, tables of findings, charts of numbers, choices to pick from.

    - Show a frame with my_air_canvas_show(folder, openui, title). folder is the canvas folder's id or its path, as my_air_folders_get gives it.
      Each call adds a new frame, the canvas shows the newest and keeps at most 50; the user can page back through older ones.
    - Read the answer of my_air_canvas_show: when validated is true and ok is false, or errors are listed, fix the document and show it again in the same turn.
      unresolved names references you never defined, orphaned statements that root does not reach (they do not show).
      When validated is false, no canvas tab is open in this IDE and the frame was stored unchecked: write it carefully, nothing will tell you about mistakes.
    - A Board's answer is in boards: every element's rectangle in grid units, as the boxes resolved, and its problems
      (overlap, overflow, anchors that do not resolve, edges through other elements...). Check the rectangles against what you meant
      and fix every problem. Heights without h are measured from the rendered content; one marked estimated is a default, since
      the page could not render in time, so give h where the size matters. An edge is checked along the path it is drawn with.
    - my_air_canvas_patch(folder, frame, patch, expect) changes a frame in place by statements: a statement of an existing name
      replaces it, a new name is added, name = null deletes it, and statements root no longer reaches are dropped. expect is the
      frame's rev from the last show, get or patch; a stale rev is refused with the current document. Patch a board you are
      building instead of showing it again, so it keeps its frame and what the user did on it, and read its answer as a show's.
    - Pass snapshot: true to show or patch when the look matters (a board, a dense layout): the answer's snapshot is the path of a
      PNG of the frame as the canvas tab shows it; Read it to see what the user sees, and fix what looks wrong.
    - my_air_canvas_get(folder, frame?) lists the frames and gives the newest frame's document and its form state, and the named
      frame's too: get an older frame this way before you patch it. my_air_canvas_apply(folder, changes)
      removes frames, retitles them or clears the canvas. my_air_canvas_language() returns these rules and the OpenUI reference again.
    - The OpenUI reference below describes the language of the openui argument only. Your replies in the terminal stay ordinary text;
      write OpenUI only inside my_air_canvas_show, never in a reply.
    - Write the whole document in one call; frames do not stream. Keep it focused: one frame answers one request.
    - Query() and Mutation() and tool calls from the canvas are not available: put the data into the document itself.
      The canvas makes no network request: images from URLs do not load, and links open only through @OpenUrl (http and https).
    - @ToAssistant(message, context) sends the message to the canvas's manager session, with the context and the submitting form's fields.
      @OpenFile(path, line) and FileRef open a file in the IDE; paths are relative to the canvas's working directory, or absolute.
""".trimIndent()

/** The first message of a canvas's manager, sent once, when its session starts with no transcript. */
const val CANVAS_MANAGER_FIRST_MESSAGE =
    "Read the canvas with my_air_canvas_get. Then tell me in one line that the canvas is ready, and wait for my request."

/**
 * The canvas manager's system prompt (`--append-system-prompt-file`, so it may span lines): the canvas it manages, named
 * by id, which a rename keeps, how clicks from the canvas arrive, then [canvasRules] and the OpenUI reference.
 */
fun canvasManagerPrompt(folderId: String, canvas: Canvas): String = """
    |You manage My Air canvas folder $folderId. Pass folder '$folderId' to my_air_canvas_show, my_air_canvas_patch, my_air_canvas_get and my_air_canvas_apply.
    |It holds ${canvas.frames.size} frames now${canvas.newest?.let { ", the newest ${it.id}" } ?: ""}; my_air_canvas_get gives the current ones.
    |Answer the user in the terminal and show results worth looking at on the canvas with my_air_canvas_show.
    |Other sessions may show frames on this canvas too; my_air_canvas_get names each frame's author session when it is known.
    |
    |A message that starts with [canvas <folder> · <frame>] is a click on frame <frame> of the canvas, not typed by the user:
    |the text after it is the button's message, and a ```json block after it carries the action's context and, for a form,
    |its fields as {"form": {"<form name>": {"<field>": value}}}. "(frame by session <id>)" names the frame's author when it is
    |not you; read that frame with my_air_canvas_get before acting on it.
    |
    |${canvasRules().replace("\n", "\n|")}
    |
    |# OpenUI Lang reference
    |
""".trimMargin() + "\n" + openUiPrompt

/** What `my_air_canvas_language` returns: [canvasRules], then the OpenUI reference. */
fun canvasLanguage(): String = canvasRules() + "\n\n# OpenUI Lang reference\n\n" + openUiPrompt
